/* book Worked example: Q: A salt has a solubility of 40 g per 100 g water at 60 °C and 20 g per 100 g water at 20 °C. 1000 kg of solution saturated at 60 °C is cooled to 20 °C. The crystals are anhydrous. Find the crystals formed and the yield (a) with no evaporation, (b) if 100 kg of water evaporates during cooling. (c) What is S when the solution first reaches 20 °C, before crystals form?
   1. Feed: solute = 1000 × 40/140 = 285.7 kg; water = 714.3 kg.
   2. (a) Water stays 714.3 kg; the mother liquor holds 714.3 × 20/100 = 142.9 kg of solute. Crystals = 285.7 − 142.9 = 142.9 kg; yield = 142.9/285.7 = 50.0 %.
   3. (b) Water left = 714.3 − 100 = 614.3 kg; solute held = 614.3 × 0.20 = 122.9 kg. Crystals = 285.7 − 122.9 = 162.9 kg; yield 57.0 %.
   4. Balance check for (b): crystals 162.9 + mother liquor (614.3 + 122.9 = 737.1) + vapour 100 = 1000 kg.
   5. (c) The cooled solution still holds 40 g per 100 g water where 20 g is the solubility: S = 40/20 = 2.0, σ = 1.0, far into the labile zone, which is why uncontrolled cooling gives a shower of fine crystals.
   Answer: 142.9 kg of crystals (50 %) without evaporation; 162.9 kg (57 %) with 100 kg evaporated; S = 2.0 if no crystals have formed by 20 °C. */
