/* book Worked example 2: Q: 1000 kg of a magnesium sulfate solution holding 40 g MgSO4 per 100 g water is cooled until the solubility is 25 g per 100 g water. The crystals are MgSO4·7H2O (MgSO4 120.37, H2O 18.015). Find the crystals formed and the yield, and compare with anhydrous crystals at the same solubility.
   1. Feed: MgSO4 = 285.7 kg; water = 714.3 kg. Hydrate molar mass = 120.37 + 7 × 18.015 = 246.48, so the crystals are 120.37/246.48 = 0.4884 MgSO4 and 0.5116 water.
   2. Let H be the hydrate formed. Water left in mother liquor = 714.3 − 0.5116H; MgSO4 left = 0.25(714.3 − 0.5116H).
   3. MgSO4 balance: 285.7 = 0.4884H + 0.25(714.3 − 0.5116H), so 285.7 − 178.6 = (0.4884 − 0.1279)H and H = 107.1/0.3605 = 297.2 kg.
   4. MgSO4 in crystals = 0.4884 × 297.2 = 145.2 kg; yield = 145.2/285.7 = 50.8 %. Check: mother liquor 702.8 kg holds 140.6 kg MgSO4 in 562.2 kg water, 25.0 g per 100 g.
   5. Anhydrous at the same solubility: 285.7 − 0.25 × 714.3 = 107.1 kg, yield 37.5 %. The 152 kg of water locked in the hydrate leaves less water to hold salt.
   Answer: 297.2 kg of MgSO4·7H2O carrying 145.2 kg of MgSO4 (50.8 % yield), against 107.1 kg (37.5 %) if the crystals were anhydrous. */
