/* book exam traps: The critical nucleus is the cluster at the top of the ΔG curve, not the smallest crystal seen: clusters smaller than r* tend to redissolve. | Secondary nucleation, not homogeneous, supplies most nuclei in a stirred plant crystallizer. | The ΔL law says all sizes grow by the same length, not by the same mass or the same percentage. | In the series model, faster stirring helps only the diffusion step; if surface integration controls, stirring barely changes G. | Nucleation order b is usually larger than growth order g, so raising supersaturation favours nucleation and gives smaller crystals. */
